    {
      "position": 3,
      "title": "Decision rights and accountability",
      "content": "Learning focus\nPolicies, committees and security roles help only when decisions have named owners and workable authority. The board sets appetite and oversight expectations; executives fund and direct action; control owners operate safeguards; independent assurance evaluates evidence. A cybersecurity team can recommend urgent containment but may need business agreement when that action will interrupt critical services. Escalation rules should define when delay is unacceptable, what facts are needed and who accepts residual risk. Accountability should survive organisational changes.\n\nWorked example: During an incident, operations wants a database service online while security recommends isolation. A predefined escalation path helps leaders compare fraud risk, service impact and reversible alternatives instead of debating authority during the crisis.\n\nPractise and reflect: Draft a simple decision-rights matrix for a suspected payment compromise. Distinguish the person who recommends, approves, executes and independently reviews each action.\n\nGovernance describes how decisions are made and held accountable. A board sets direction and oversight expectations, executives allocate resources and manage risk, operational owners run controls, and independent assurance assesses whether evidence supports management's claims. Roles vary by organisation, but decision rights must be clear before an emergency. A security officer who identifies an urgent risk may lack authority to suspend a revenue-critical service without business coordination. Conversely, a business leader should not dismiss technical evidence without a transparent risk decision. Written responsibilities are useful only when they reflect how people actually act.\n\nMap who recommends, who authorises, who executes and who reviews. These responsibilities may be separate to reduce conflicts and maintain auditability. For example, an incident responder might recommend isolating a server; the service owner confirms operational implications; an authorised crisis leader approves interruption; technical staff implement the change; independent assurance later evaluates the decision and its evidence. This is not bureaucracy for its own sake. It prevents indecision caused by unclear authority and provides a record of why a particular trade-off was made.\n\nWorked scenario: during suspected fraudulent activity, cybersecurity recommends disabling a payment connection. Operations warns that a full block would stop all customer settlements. Without a predefined path, the teams debate hierarchy while exposure continues. With an escalation plan, they present options: restrict the suspicious source, activate additional review, switch traffic or temporarily suspend a subset of transactions. The authorised leader selects an option based on confirmed facts and residual risk. Accountability means recording the decision and its basis, not retrospectively blaming whichever team raised the concern.\n\nPractice designing an incident decision matrix for a fictional financial institution. Include a low-impact monitoring alert, suspected customer-data exposure and confirmed transaction-integrity failure. Identify the escalation trigger and approving authority for each. Define how disagreement is resolved when time is limited. Explain the difference between owning the risk and performing a technical task to address it. At the end, list evidence an internal auditor could use to confirm that the agreed decision process operated as intended."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "title": "Select controls for a stated objective",
      "content": "Learning focus\nControls are methods of changing risk, not achievements merely because they appear in an inventory. Preventive controls lower the likelihood of some events; detective controls make emerging harm visible; responsive and recovery controls reduce consequences. Design the control around a measurable objective, with a clear owner, reliable evidence and frequency. Two tools that fail in the same way are less resilient than independent safeguards. Control effectiveness must consider bypasses, maintenance and response readiness, not procurement status.\n\nWorked example: A firm reports that backups run nightly. Auditors ask whether restoration of the actual critical service has been tested, including identities, dependencies and transaction reconciliation. The backup schedule alone cannot answer the recovery question.\n\nPractise and reflect: Take a risk scenario and propose one preventive, one detective and one recovery control. Define a test that could show each control does not work as intended.\n\nControls must be linked to objectives rather than treated as evidence of maturity merely because technology was purchased. A preventive safeguard may limit which users can perform an action; a detective safeguard may identify an unexpected pattern; a responsive safeguard may stop ongoing harm; a recovery safeguard may restore a service. Each needs a defined owner, purpose, operating conditions and a method of demonstrating effectiveness. Tool count says little about whether an organisation can sustain a control during incidents, organisational changes or supplier interruptions.\n\nDistinguish design effectiveness from operation. A policy that requires multifactor authentication may be well designed, but a test must establish whether it applies to the relevant accounts and whether exceptions are controlled. A backup schedule may show activity, while an actual restoration test shows whether dependent services and data can recover. Independent safeguards should fail differently where practical; two dashboards relying on the same missing log source do not provide independent detection. Testing should consider exceptions, human decisions and failure recovery, not merely a green configuration icon.\n\nWorked example: management states that disaster recovery is complete because backup jobs succeed every night. An assurance review discovers that the application requires a network route, identity service and database consistency procedure absent from the recovery test. The company may have usable backup files while lacking a proven recoverable business service. Management therefore schedules an integrated restoration exercise, records dependencies, verifies transaction integrity and reviews the recovery time experienced. The more meaningful measure is the service outcome achieved under controlled conditions.\n\nPractice: select a risk concerning unauthorised payment changes. Propose a preventive control such as role-based approval, a detective control such as reconciled exception reporting and a recovery control such as controlled correction with audit trails. Define test evidence for each and explain a way it might fail despite appearing installed. If an exception exists, name the authority required to accept it and the condition triggering reassessment. End by explaining why a control is only valuable when it reduces a credible business risk."
    },
